1998 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The \042Golden Hour\042 refers to the first sixty minutes immediately after a traumatic injury\054 a critical window during which timely medical intervention can\015significantly improve survival outcomes\056 During this period\054 rapid stabilisation\015and access to care are essential\054 as the body is highly vulnerable to complications such as internal bleeding or organ failure\056 However\054 as highlighted in the study\054 delays in reaching medical facilities remain a major concern\054 with nearly 50\045 of patients at risk due to lack of timely care and around 30\045 of deaths linked directly to delayed emergency response\056\015\n\nThese delays\054 often caused by traffic congestion and limitations in existing ambulance systems\054 reduce the effectiveness of treatment and turn otherwise survivable conditions into fatal ones\056 Therefore\054 preserving the Golden Hour is crucial\054 making response time the most decisive factor in emergency care and emphasising the urgent need for faster\054 more efficient medical transport systems\056)
     /K [ 10 ]
     /P 1999 0 R
     /Pg 1978 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

